      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.3$ | $20$ |\n| $0.6$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n\nGiven the table above, select the options that make the following statement true:\n\nThe variable $y$ is {{field-1}} proportional to $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=$  {{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "If $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k.$ Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y=k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute $x\\cdot y=k$ for each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y=k$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.3$ | $20$ | $0.3\\cdot 20=6$ |\n| $0.6$ | $10$ | $0.6\\cdot 10=6$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2\\cdot 3=6$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=6.$ Therefore, we conclude:\n\nThe variable $y$ is $\\text{inversely}$ proportional  to $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=6.$",
